In C++, you can use a macro to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. Macros are defined using the `#define` directive. Here's an example of how you can create a macro to assign a value to multiple variables:

```cpp
#define ASSIGN_VALUE(x, y, z) x = y; y = z; z = x;

int main() {
    int a, b, c;
    ASSIGN_VALUE(a, 10, 20);
    ASSIGN_VALUE(b, 30, 40);
    ASSIGN_VALUE(c, 50, 60);

    // Now a, b, and c have the values 20, 40, and 60 respectively
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `ASSIGN_VALUE` macro takes three arguments and assigns the second argument to the first, the third to the second, and the first to the third. When you call `ASSIGN_VALUE(a, 10, 20);`, it effectively does `a = 10; b = 20; c = 10;`. This way, you can run the same code inline on multiple named variables using the macro.

