Certainly! Below is a Java method that takes a `List<Car>` as an argument and returns a `Map<String, Integer>` where the key is the color of the most popular car and the value is the count of that color. If there are multiple colors with the same highest count, it will return the first one encountered.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Collections;

public class CarColorCounter {

    public static Map<String, Integer> getMostPopularCarColor(List<Car> cars) {
        Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap = new HashMap<>();

        // Count the occurrences of each car color
        for (Car car : cars) {
            String color = car.getColor();
            colorCountMap.put(color, colorCountMap.getOrDefault(color, 0) + 1);
        }

        // Find the most popular color
        String mostPopularColor = Collections.max(colorCountMap.entrySet(), Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).getKey();
        int mostPopularCount = Collections.max(colorCountMap.entrySet(), Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).getValue();

        // Return a map with the most popular color and its count
        Map<String, Integer> result = new HashMap<>();
        result.put(mostPopularColor, mostPopularCount);

        return result;
    }
}
```

This method first creates a `HashMap` to count the occurrences of each car color. It then uses the `Collections.max` method to find the entry with the highest value (count) in the map. Finally, it returns a new `HashMap` containing the most popular color and its count.

